# What NOT to upload to EAS Build.
#
# The first cloud build reported:
#
#     Your project archive is 524 MB
#
# for an app whose source is a few megabytes. eas-cli takes a shallow COPY of
# the working tree (cli.requireCommit is unset — see SHIPPING.md) and filters
# it, and the filtering was not excluding `android/`.
#
# Measured rather than assumed:
#
#     android/       1.9 GB    Gradle caches, merged manifests, intermediates
#     node_modules/  912 MB    (already excluded)
#     assets/        572 KB
#     .expo/         633 KB
#
# 1.9 GB of local Android build output is the entire story — it compresses to
# the 524 MB that was being uploaded on every build, and it is what stalled two
# rebuilds mid-upload.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING OR REMOVING A LINE.
#
# When .easignore exists, EAS uses it INSTEAD OF .gitignore — not in addition
# to it. Every protection .gitignore was providing for the upload disappears
# the moment this file is created, and it disappears silently.
#
# The one that matters is `.env`. SHIPPING.md's whole STEP ZERO section rests
# on `.env` never reaching EAS, and until this file existed that was true only
# because .gitignore excluded it. Creating .easignore without re-stating it
# would have uploaded it — quietly, with no warning, on the next build.
#
# So the secret patterns below are duplicated from .gitignore ON PURPOSE. They
# are not redundant. Deleting one does not fall back to .gitignore; it ships
# the file.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- Secrets and credentials: the reason this file is dangerous ---------------
.env
.env.*
*.jks
*.p8
*.p12
*.key
*.mobileprovision
google-services.json
play-service-account.json

# --- Native projects ----------------------------------------------------------
# Generated by prebuild, and EAS runs prebuild itself for a CNG project (this
# app configures iOS entirely through app.json plugins — the first successful
# iOS build had no ios/ directory at all). Uploading a locally-generated copy is
# both wasteful and a way to ship a stale native config that disagrees with
# app.json.
android/
ios/

# --- Dependencies and build output ---------------------------------------------
# EAS installs dependencies on the worker from the lockfile; a local
# node_modules is never used and may hold the wrong platform's binaries.
node_modules/
.expo/
dist/
web-build/
.kotlin/
*.log

# --- Things a build has no use for ---------------------------------------------
# Kept in the repo, irrelevant to compiling the app.
.maestro/
store/

# Test files are deliberately NOT excluded. They are a rounding error next to
# android/, and excluding them risks a build-time step that walks the tsconfig
# include set finding a dangling reference. The size problem is the native
# directories; solve that and stop.
